---
import BlogLayout from "../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { isPublished } from "../utils/posts";

// The site had no About page at all: no page explaining who builds the app or
// why they are worth trusting on renovation. That is the weakest E-E-A-T
// signal a site can send, and it is the page every Article's author URL should
// resolve to. See the 2026-08-27 SEO audit.
const posts = await getCollection("blog", ({ data }) => isPublished(data));
const siteUrl = Astro.site?.href ?? "https://home-stories.12f.dk";
const canonicalPath = "/about/";

const personSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  "@id": new URL("/about/#robert-jensen", Astro.site).href,
  name: "Robert Jensen",
  url: new URL(canonicalPath, Astro.site).href,
  email: "robert@12f.dk",
  jobTitle: "Software developer and renovator",
  nationality: "Danish",
  description:
    "Danish software developer who built Home Stories after renovating his own kitchen, bathroom and attic, and running each project out of a spreadsheet that stopped being useful by week two.",
  knowsAbout: [
    "Home renovation planning",
    "Renovation budgeting and contingency",
    "Construction project sequencing",
    "Renovation documentation and insurance evidence",
    "iOS application development",
  ],
  worksFor: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "12f",
    url: "https://www.12f.dk",
  },
  sameAs: [
    "https://www.robert-jensen.dk",
    "https://github.com/12fdk",
    "https://apps.apple.com/developer/id1786807724",
  ],
};

const aboutSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "AboutPage",
  url: new URL(canonicalPath, Astro.site).href,
  name: "About Home Stories and Robert Jensen",
  description:
    "Who builds Home Stories, why it exists, how it is funded, and the experience behind the renovation guides published on this site.",
  inLanguage: "en",
  mainEntity: { "@id": new URL("/about/#robert-jensen", Astro.site).href },
  isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", url: siteUrl },
};
---

<BlogLayout
  title="About Home Stories — Who Builds It and Why"
  description="Home Stories is built by Robert Jensen, a Danish developer who renovated his own kitchen, bathroom and attic first. Who writes the guides, how the app is funded, and what it will never do."
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  extraSchema={[personSchema, aboutSchema]}
>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 md:py-16">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-6 text-sm text-base-content/60">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a>
      <span class="mx-1.5" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span>About</span>
    </nav>

    <header class="mb-10">
      <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-bold leading-tight md:text-5xl">
        About Home Stories
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-base-content/80" data-speakable>
        Home Stories is a renovation tracker for iPhone, built and maintained by
        one person: Robert Jensen, a Danish software developer who renovated a
        kitchen, a bathroom and an attic before writing a line of it. The app
        exists because every tool he tried — spreadsheets, Notion, generic
        project managers — stopped being useful somewhere around week two.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <h2>Who writes the guides</h2>
      <p>
        Every article on this site is written by Robert Jensen. They are not
        commissioned, syndicated, or generated on someone else's behalf. The
        numbers in them come from three sources, and the articles say which:
        published industry research (cited inline), quoted work from Danish and
        UK contractors, and direct experience of running the projects above.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a figure is a range rather than a number, that is deliberate —
        renovation costs and durations vary enormously by region, scope and the
        state of the building. An article that gives you one confident number
        for "how long a bathroom takes" is guessing.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the app exists</h2>
      <p>
        A renovation generates three streams of information at once: what you
        are spending, what still has to happen and in what order, and what the
        place looked like before you closed the wall. Most people track the
        first in a spreadsheet, the second in their head, and the third in a
        camera roll they cannot search. Home Stories keeps all three attached
        to the same project, which is the only reason any of it stays useful
        six months later when the insurer asks.
      </p>

      <h2>How it is funded</h2>
      <p>
        The app is free, and it stays free for the whole job — unlimited
        projects, tasks, phases, photos and expense logging. There is one
        optional in-app purchase that unlocks the deeper budget analysis, CSV
        and PDF export, and task notifications. That single purchase is the
        entire business model.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no subscription, no advertising, no analytics SDK inside the
        app, and no data sold to anyone. Projects live on your device and sync
        only to your own iCloud account if you turn that on. The
        <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a> spells out the details.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site will not do</h2>
      <p>
        The guides are here because they are worth reading on their own. They
        mention the app where it is genuinely the tool for the job and not
        otherwise — several of them recommend a spreadsheet, a printed
        checklist, or a competitor if that fits better. If an article ever reads
        like an advert, it has failed at the thing it was written for.
      </p>

      <h2>Get in touch</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections, disagreements and bug reports are all welcome, and
        corrections to published articles are made with a visible updated date.
        Email <a href="mailto:robert@12f.dk">robert@12f.dk</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The details</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>App</strong>: Home Stories: Renovation App, iPhone, iOS 17.0 or later</li>
        <li><strong>Developer</strong>: Robert Jensen, trading as 12f, Denmark</li>
        <li><strong>Published guides</strong>: {posts.length} articles, all written in-house</li>
        <li><strong>Contact</strong>: <a href="mailto:robert@12f.dk">robert@12f.dk</a></li>
        <li><strong>Elsewhere</strong>:
          <a href="https://www.robert-jensen.dk" rel="me">robert-jensen.dk</a>,
          <a href="https://github.com/12fdk" rel="me">GitHub</a>,
          <a href="https://apps.apple.com/developer/id1786807724" rel="me">App Store</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </main>
</BlogLayout>
